==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|astrologer}}, {{m|enm|astrologier}}, {{m|enm|astrologere}}, formed from {{der|en|enm|astrologie}} or {{der|en|la|astrologus}} + {{der|en|enm|-ere}}, equivalent to {{af|en|astrology|-er|id2=occupation}}. Ousted imported {{noncog|enm|astrologien|t=astrologer}}, borrowed from {{noncog|fro|astrologien}}. {{displaced|ang|tungolwītga|lit=star prophet}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈstɹɒl.ə.d͡ʒɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Arlo Barnes-astrologer.wav}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who studies or practices [[astrology]].
#: {{ux|en|Nicolaus Copernicus and Johannes Kepler both made a living as '''astrologers'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author={{w|Keith Thomas (historian)|Keith Thomas}}|title=Religion and the Decline of Magic|page=335|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2012
|passage=When witchcraft became a statutory offence again in 1563, '''astrologers''' reincurred the risk of prosecution {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1981|author=w:William Irwin Thompson|title=The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture|publisher=Rider/Hutchinson & Co.|location=London|page=130|passage='''Astrologers''', when they observe this pattern, insist that it corresponds to the zodiacal progression. I am not an '''astrologer''' and I hold no brief for it, but I do believe the origins of astrology are not with the Chaldeans of Mesopotamia but with the hunters and gatherers of the Stone Age.}}

